## DESCRIPTION
## Trigonometric Functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Triangle trigonometry)
## DBsection(Applications of special triangles & right triangles)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## Level(5)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Functions Modeling Change')
## AuthorText1('Connally')
## EditionText1('3')
## Section1(6.Tools)
## Problem1(22)
## KEYWORDS('trig')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',            'PGML.pl',
    'parserNumberWithUnits.pl', 'contextTrigDegrees.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('TrigDegrees');

$zyard = random(50, 100, 50);
$z     = 3 * $zyard;            # z in feet
do { $theta = random(10, 40, 1); } until $theta != 30;

$ansy = NumberWithUnits("$z sin($theta)", "ft");

BEGIN_PGML
A kite flier wondered how high her kite was flying.  She used a protractor to measure an angle of [` [$theta]^{\circ} `] from level ground to the kite string.  If she used a full [` [$zyard] `] yard spool of string, how high, in feet was the kite?  (Disregard
the string sag and assume that the string reel was at ground height.)  Include units in your answer.  All trig functions will be evaluated in degrees.

Height = [_]{$ansy}

[@  helpLink('units')  @]*
END_PGML

COMMENT("Uses trig functions to be in degrees (instead of the default radians)"
);
ENDDOCUMENT();
